+++
title: Thebes, Egypt
description: Ancient Egyptian city
hook: By 1500 BC Thebes may have been the largest city in the world — about
  75,000 people, on the historian Ian Morris's estimate — and it held that
  place until roughly 900 BC, when Nimrud and others passed it.
standfirst: "An ancient Egyptian city on the Nile, 800 kilometers south of the
  Mediterranean, and the capital of Egypt for long periods of the Middle and
  New Kingdoms: temples on the eastern bank, a necropolis of private and royal
  cemeteries on the western, and, since 1979, a UNESCO World Heritage Site in
  what is now Luxor."
key-facts:
  - name: Where it is
    value: Luxor, Luxor Governorate, Egypt, in Upper Egypt
    from: "location: Luxor, Luxor Governorate, Egypt region: Upper Egypt"
  - name: What is left
    value: In ruins
    from: "site-notes: condition: In ruins"
  - name: Its Egyptian name
    value: Waset
    from: known to the [[ ancient Egyptians | ancient-egypt ]] as !!Waset!!
  - name: Capital of Egypt
    value: For long periods during the Middle Kingdom and New Kingdom eras
    from: Thebes was the main city of the fourth Upper Egyptian [[ nome |
      nome-egypt ]] (Sceptre nome) and was the capital of Egypt for long
      periods during the [[ Middle Kingdom | middle-kingdom-of-egypt ]] and [[
      New Kingdom | new-kingdom-of-egypt ]] eras.
  - name: How big
    value: 93 km^^2^^ (36 sq mi)
    from: Thebes had an area of 93 km^^2^^ (36 sq mi), which included parts of the
      Theban Hills in the west that culminates at the sacred 420-meter
      (1,380-foot) [[ al-Qurn ]].
  - name: World Heritage listing
    value: '"Ancient Thebes with its Necropolis", a Cultural site, designated 1979
      (3rd session)'
    from: 'unesco-world-heritage-site: official-name: Ancient Thebes with its
      Necropolis type: Cultural criteria: I, III, VI designated: 1979 (3rd
      session) reference-no: "87" region: Egypt'
pull-quotes:
  - text: \... in Egyptian Thebes the heaps of precious ingots gleam, the
      hundred-gated Thebes.
    attribution: Homer, //Iliad//, Book 9 (c. 8th Century BC)
    section: economy
  - text: This city, the whole of it, I conquered it with the help of Ashur and
      Ishtar. Silver, gold, precious stones, all the wealth of the palace,
      rich cloth, precious linen, great horses, supervising men and women, two
      obelisks of splendid electrum, weighing 2,500 talents, the doors of
      temples I tore from their bases and carried them off to Assyria.
    attribution: Ashurbanipal
    section: late-period
timeline:
  - when: 3200 BC
    what: Thebes is inhabited — still a small trading post, while Memphis serves
      as the royal residence of the Old Kingdom pharaohs.
    from: Thebes was inhabited from around 3200 BC.[[ ^18 ]] It was the eponymous
      capital of Waset, the fourth [[ Upper Egyptian | upper-egypt ]] [[ nome
      | nome-egypt ]]. At this time it was still a small trading post, while
      [[ Memphis | memphis-egypt ]] served as the royal residence of the [[
      Old Kingdom ]] pharaohs.
  - when: 2160 BC
    what: A rival line based at Thebes, the Eleventh Dynasty, rules the remaining
      part of Upper Egypt against the pharaohs at Herakleopolis Magna.
    from: By 2160 BC, a new line of pharaohs (the [[ Ninth |
      ninth-dynasty-of-egypt ]] and [[ Tenth | tenth-dynasty-of-egypt ]]
      Dynasties) consolidated control over [[ Lower Egypt ]] and northern
      parts of [[ Upper Egypt ]] from their capital in [[ Herakleopolis Magna
      ]]. A rival line (the [[ Eleventh Dynasty | eleventh-dynasty-of-egypt
      ]]), based at Thebes, ruled the remaining part of Upper Egypt.
  - when: c. 2050 BC
    what: Mentuhotep II takes the Herakleopolitans by force and reunites Egypt,
      starting the Middle Kingdom.
    from: Finally by c. 2050 BC, [[ Intef III ]]'s son [[ Mentuhotep II ]]
      (meaning "Montu is satisfied"), took the Herakleopolitans by force and
      reunited Egypt once again under one ruler, thereby starting the period
      now known as the [[ Middle Kingdom | middle-kingdom-of-egypt ]].
  - when: 2000 BC
    what: About 40,000 inhabitants, against 60,000 at Memphis, then the largest
      city in the world, according to George Modelski.
    from: According to [[ George Modelski ]], Thebes had about 40,000 inhabitants
      in 2000 BC (compared to 60,000 in [[ Memphis | memphis-egypt ]], the
      largest city in the world at the time).
  - when: 1800 BC
    what: Memphis is down to about 30,000, making Thebes the largest city in Egypt
      at the time.
    from: By 1800 BC, the population of Memphis was down to about 30,000, making
      Thebes the largest city in Egypt at the time.[[ ^12 ]]
  - when: c. 1700 BC
    what: The Hyksos take Memphis; the rulers of the 13th Dynasty flee south to
      Thebes, which is restored as capital.
    from: When the Hyksos took Memphis during or shortly after [[ Merneferre Ay
      ]]'s reign (c. 1700 BC), the rulers of the 13th Dynasty fled south to
      Thebes, which was restored as capital.[[ ^23 ]]
  - when: 1657–1549 BC
    what: The Second Intermediate Period; the Hyksos kings gain the upper hand
      over Lower Egypt early in it.
    from: The Hyksos kings gained the upper hand over Lower Egypt early into the
      [[ Second Intermediate Period ]] (1657–1549 BC).[[ ^22 ]]
  - when: 1506–1493 BC
    what: Thutmose I begins the first great expansion of the Karnak temple.
    from: "[[ Thutmose I ]] (reigned 1506–1493 BC) began the first great expansion
      of the [[ Karnak ]] temple."
  - when: 1500 BC
    what: Thebes may have grown to be the largest city in the world, with a
      population of about 75,000, on Ian Morris's estimate.
    from: Historian [[ Ian Morris | ian-morris-historian ]] has estimated that by
      1500 BC, Thebes may have grown to be the largest city in the world, with
      a population of about 75,000, a position it held until about 900 BC,
      when it was surpassed by [[ Nimrud ]] (among others).[[ ^13 ]]
  - when: 1479–1458 BC
    what: Hatshepsut renews trade networks, primarily the Red Sea trade with the
      land of Punt.
    from: Queen [[ Hatshepsut ]] (reigned 1479–1458 BC) helped the Theban economy
      flourish by renewing trade networks, primarily the Red Sea trade between
      Thebes's Red Sea port of [[ Al-Qusayr | al-qusayr-egypt ]], [[ Elat |
      eilat ]] and the [[ land of Punt ]].
  - when: 1388–1350 BC
    what: The 18th Dynasty reaches its peak under Amenhotep III, who builds the
      mortuary temple and the Malkata palace-city on the west bank and the
      Luxor temple in the city proper.
    from: The 18th Dynasty reached its peak during his great-grandson [[ Amenhotep
      III ]]'s reign (1388–1350 BC). Aside from embellishing the temples of
      Amun, Amenhotep increased construction in Thebes to unprecedented
      levels. On the west bank, he built the enormous [[ mortuary temple |
      mortuary-temple-of-amenhotep-iii ]] and the equally massive [[ Malkata
      ]] palace-city, which fronted a 364-hectare artificial lake. In the city
      proper he built the [[ Luxor temple ]] and the Avenue of the Sphinxes
      leading to Karnak.
  - when: 1351–1334 BC
    what: Under Akhenaten the court abandons Thebes and the worship of Amun is
      proscribed; the capital moves to Akhetaten.
    from: For a brief period in the reign of Amenhotep III's son [[ Akhenaten ]]
      (1351–1334 BC), Thebes fell on hard times; the city was abandoned by the
      court, and the worship of Amun was proscribed. The capital was moved to
      the new city of [[ Akhetaten | amarna ]] (Amarna in modern Egypt),
      midway between Thebes and Memphis.
  - when: 1187–1156 BC
    what: The Great Harris Papyrus states that Ramesses III donated 86,486 slaves
      and vast estates to the temples of Amun.
    from: The [[ Great Harris Papyrus | papyrus-harris-i ]] states that [[
      Ramesses III ]] (reigned 1187–1156 BC) donated 86,486 slaves and vast
      estates to the temples of Amun.
  - when: about 1114 BC
    what: Investigations into the plundering of royal tombs in the necropolis of
      western Thebes uncover proof of corruption in high places.
    from: During the reign of [[ Ramesses IX ]] (1129–1111 BC), about 1114 BC, a
      series of investigations into the plundering of royal tombs in the
      necropolis of western Thebes uncovered proof of corruption in high
      places, following an accusation made by the mayor of the east bank
      against his colleague on the west.
  - when: about 900 BC
    what: Thebes is surpassed by Nimrud (among others).
    from: Historian [[ Ian Morris | ian-morris-historian ]] has estimated that by
      1500 BC, Thebes may have grown to be the largest city in the world, with
      a population of about 75,000, a position it held until about 900 BC,
      when it was surpassed by [[ Nimrud ]] (among others).[[ ^13 ]]
  - when: around 750 BC
    what: The Kushites (Nubians) are growing their influence over Thebes and Upper
      Egypt.
    from: By around 750 BC, the [[ Kushites ]] (Nubians) were growing their
      influence over Thebes and Upper Egypt.
  - when: 721 BC
    what: Shabaka of the Kushites reunifies Egypt and makes Thebes the capital of
      his kingdom.
    from: In 721 BC, King [[ Shabaka ]] of the Kushites defeated the combined
      forces of [[ Osorkon IV ]] ([[ 22nd Dynasty ]]), [[ Peftjauawybast ]]
      ([[ 23rd Dynasty ]]) [[ Bakenranef ]] ([[ 24th Dynasty |
      twenty-fourth-dynasty-of-egypt ]]) and reunified Egypt yet again. His
      reign saw a significant amount of building work undertaken throughout
      Egypt, especially at the city of Thebes, which he made the capital of
      his kingdom.
  - when: 667 BC
    what: Attacked by Ashurbanipal's army, Taharqa abandons Lower Egypt and flees
      to Thebes.
    from: In 667 BC, attacked by the [[ Assyrian | assyria ]] king [[ Ashurbanipal
      ]]'s army, Taharqa abandoned Lower Egypt and fled to Thebes.
  - when: 663 BC
    what: Tantamani abandons his attempt to conquer the country; the Assyrians
      pursue him and take Thebes.
    from: After his death three years later his nephew (or cousin) [[ Tantamani ]]
      seized Thebes, invaded Lower Egypt and laid siege to Memphis, but
      abandoned his attempts to conquer the country in 663 BC and retreated
      southwards.[[ ^34 ]] The Assyrians pursued him and [[ took Thebes |
      sack-of-thebes ]], whose name was added to a long list of cities
      plundered and destroyed by the Assyrians
  - when: 656 BC
    what: Psamtik I, installed by the Assyrians, ascends to Thebes and has his
      daughter Nitocris I adopted as heiress to God's Wife of Amun.
    from: Assyrians installed [[ Psamtik I ]] (664–610 BC), who ascended to Thebes
      in 656 BC and brought about the adoption of his own daughter, [[
      Nitocris I ]], as heiress to God's Wife of Amun there.
  - when: 525 BC
    what: Cambyses II invades Egypt and becomes pharaoh, subordinating the kingdom
      as a satrapy to the Achaemenid Empire.
    from: In 525 BC, Persian [[ Cambyses II ]] invaded Egypt and became pharaoh,
      subordinating the kingdom as a [[ satrapy ]] to the greater [[
      Achaemenid Empire ]].[[ ^36 ]]
  - when: 185 BC
    what: Ankhmakis's revolt is suppressed; Ptolemy V, in need of the priesthood's
      support, pardons the Thebans who backed it.
    from: His successor, [[ Ankhmakis ]], held large parts of Upper Egypt until
      185 BC. This revolt was supported by the Theban priesthood. After the
      suppression of the revolt in 185 BC, [[ Ptolemy V ]], in need of the
      support of the priesthood, pardoned them.
  - when: 132 BC
    what: The Thebans rise again and elevate Harsiesi to the throne; he empties
      the royal bank and flees the following year.
    from: Half a century later the Thebans rose again, elevating a certain [[
      Harsiesi ]] to the throne in 132 BC. Harsiesi, having helped himself to
      the funds of the royal bank at Thebes, fled the following year.
  - when: 91 BC
    what: Another revolt breaks out; in the following years Thebes is subdued and
      the city turned into rubble.
    from: In 91 BC, another revolt broke out. In the following years, Thebes was
      subdued, and the city turned into rubble.[[ ^37 ]]
  - when: first century AD
    what: Strabo describes Thebes as having been relegated to a mere village.
    from: In the first century AD, [[ Strabo ]] described Thebes as having been
      relegated to a mere village.[[ ^27 ]]
  - when: "1979"
    what: UNESCO classifies the ruins of ancient Thebes as a World Heritage Site.
    from: In 1979, the ruins of ancient Thebes were classified by [[ UNESCO ]] as
      a [[ World Heritage Site ]].[[ ^1 ]]
