glossary:
  - term: Nome
    gloss: One of the districts of Egypt; Thebes was the main city of the fourth
      Upper Egyptian nome, the Sceptre nome.
    from: Thebes was the main city of the fourth Upper Egyptian [[ nome |
      nome-egypt ]] (Sceptre nome)
  - term: wꜣs
    gloss: The sceptre of the pharaohs, a long staff with an animal's head and a
      forked base; the Egyptian name for Thebes, //wꜣs.t//, meant "City of the
      wꜣs".
    from: The [[ Egyptian | egyptian-language ]] name for Thebes was //wꜣs.t//,
      "City of the [[ //wꜣs// | was-sceptre ]]", the [[ sceptre ]] of the [[
      pharaohs | pharaoh ]], a long staff with an animal's head and a forked
      base.
  - term: Theban Triad
    gloss: "The three deities of the city: Amun as chief, with Mut and Khonsu."
    from: the "City of [[ Amun ]]", the chief of the [[ Theban Triad ]] of deities
      whose other members were [[ Mut ]] and [[ Khonsu ]]
  - term: Necropolis
    gloss: "The burial ground on the western bank: large private and royal
      cemeteries and funerary complexes."
    from: the western bank, where a [[ necropolis of large private and royal
      cemeteries and funerary complexes | theban-necropolis ]] can be found
  - term: Wadi
    gloss: A valley of the Eastern Desert draining into the Nile valley; Wadi
      Hammamat was used as an overland trade route to the Red Sea coast.
    from: In the east lies the mountainous [[ Eastern Desert ]] with its [[ wadis
      | wadi ]] draining into the valley. Significant among these wadis is [[
      Wadi Hammamat ]] near Thebes. It was used as an overland trade route
      going to the [[ Red Sea ]] coast.
  - term: Hyksos
    gloss: From //Heqa-khasut//, "rulers of foreign lands" — what the Egyptians
      called the leaders of a second wave of Asiatics, who overran Avaris and
      founded the 15th Dynasty there.
    from: A second wave of [[ Asiatics | middle-east ]] called [[ Hyksos ]] (from
      //Heqa-khasut//, "rulers of foreign lands" as Egyptians called their
      leaders) immigrated into Egypt and overran the Canaanite center of power
      at Avaris, starting the [[ 15th Dynasty | fifteenth-dynasty-of-egypt ]]
      there.
  - term: Diospolis
    gloss: "\"City of Zeus\", the Greek translation of the city's name, made after
      Amun was rendered as Zeus Ammon."
    from: In the //[[ interpretatio graeca ]]//, Amun was rendered as [[ Zeus
      Ammon ]]. The name was therefore translated into Greek as Diospolis,
      "City of Zeus".
  - term: God's Wife of Amun
    gloss: A Theban office in which daughters of the Tanite kings were installed,
      and in which they wielded greater power.
    from: daughters of the Tanite kings being installed as God's Wife of Amun at
      Thebes, where they wielded greater power
further:
  - slug: memphis-egypt
    why: "The rival capital: 60,000 people when Thebes had 40,000, and the royal
      residence of the Old Kingdom pharaohs while Thebes was still a trading
      post."
  - slug: amarna
    why: Akhetaten, the city Akhenaten built midway between Thebes and Memphis
      when the court abandoned Thebes and the worship of Amun was proscribed.
  - slug: nimrud
    why: The city that, on Ian Morris's estimate, surpassed Thebes as the largest
      in the world around 900 BC.
anchors:
  hook: Historian [[ Ian Morris | ian-morris-historian ]] has estimated that by
    1500 BC, Thebes may have grown to be the largest city in the world, with a
    population of about 75,000, a position it held until about 900 BC, when it
    was surpassed by [[ Nimrud ]] (among others).[[ ^13 ]]
  standfirst: >-
    !!Thebes!! (Egyptian Arabic: طيبه), known to the [[ ancient Egyptians |
    ancient-egypt ]] as !!Waset!!, was an [[ ancient Egyptian city |
    list-of-ancient-egyptian-sites ]] located along the [[ Nile ]] about 800
    kilometers (500 mi) south of the [[ Mediterranean ]]. Its ruins lie within
    the modern [[ Egyptian | egypt ]] [[ city | list-of-egyptian-cities ]] of
    [[ Luxor ]]. Thebes was the main city of the fourth Upper Egyptian [[ nome
    | nome-egypt ]] (Sceptre nome) and was the capital of Egypt for long
    periods during the [[ Middle Kingdom | middle-kingdom-of-egypt ]] and [[
    New Kingdom | new-kingdom-of-egypt ]] eras.


    The city was close to [[ Nubia ]] and the [[ Eastern Desert ]], with its
    valuable mineral resources and trade routes. It was a [[ religious center
    | theban-triad ]] and the most venerated city during many periods of
    ancient Egyptian history.


    The site of Thebes includes areas on both the eastern bank of the Nile,
    where the temples of [[ Karnak ]] and [[ Luxor | luxor-temple ]] stand and
    where the city was situated; and the western bank, where a [[ necropolis
    of large private and royal cemeteries and funerary complexes |
    theban-necropolis ]] can be found. In 1979, the ruins of ancient Thebes
    were classified by [[ UNESCO ]] as a [[ World Heritage Site ]].[[ ^1 ]]
